{"run_id": "h1-ds-b12-native-r1", "seconds": 21.426, "memory_files": ["MEMORY.md"], "error": null, "result_keys": ["final_response", "last_reasoning", "messages", "api_calls", "completed", "turn_exit_reason", "failed", "partial", "interrupted", "response_transformed", "pre_transform_response", "response_previewed", "model", "requested_model", "served_model", "provider", "base_url", "input_tokens", "output_tokens", "cache_read_tokens", "cache_write_tokens", "reasoning_tokens", "prompt_tokens", "completion_tokens", "total_tokens", "last_prompt_tokens", "estimated_cost_usd", "cost_status", "cost_source", "service_tier", "session_id", "turn_id", "current_turn_user_idx"], "final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"answer\": 8,\n  \"sql\": \"SELECT COUNT(*) AS answer FROM (SELECT i.ref_id FROM items_g2 i JOIN fdbk_g2 f ON f.ref_id = i.ref_id WHERE i.prc_usd > 50 GROUP BY i.ref_id HAVING COUNT(*) >= 5 AND AVG(f.rtg) < 3.0);\"\n}\n```\n\n**How I got there:** `items_g2`/`fdbk_g2` is the electronics dataset (its `main_cat` values are Computers, All Electronics, Camera & Photo, Cell Phones & Accessories, etc.). Price is `prc_usd` (USD) / `prc` (cents) \u2014 both filters return the same 8. Reviews join on `fdbk_g2.ref_id \u2192 items_g2.ref_id` (not `item_id`), rating is `fdbk_g2.rtg`. Grouping per product with `HAVING COUNT(*)>=5 AND AVG(rtg)<3.0` yields 8 products.\n\nOne note: `items_g2.avg_rtg` (the stored per-product rating) is inconsistent with the review table \u2014 using it instead gives 3. Since the question asks for products with \"at least 5 reviews and an average review rating below 3.0,\" I computed both the count and the average from the review table, which is self-consistent (answer 8)."}
